  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/... \nThe strong pressure gradient and resultant strong easterly winds \nacross southern AZ will weaken heading into Sunday as the strong\nsurface high in the Plains weakens and shifts to the Mid-Atlantic.\nThere will still be some easterly breeziness, but nowhere near the\nmagnitude of Friday through Saturday. The synoptic pattern will\nalso shift, with the ridge over the Northwest CONUS sliding east\nthrough the Plains and the cutoff low will also weaken into an\nopen wave and lift through the area Sunday-Monday, downstream of\nthe next Fall trough digging down the West Coast.\n\nLingering light showers are possible across portions of AZ, mainly\neast and southeast AZ, Sunday into the start of next week \ndownstream of the lingering shortwave trough, but drier air is \nshown to move in aloft prior to the arrival of the next coastal\ntrough. Temperatures should warm Sunday into the start of next\nweek with the latest NBM showing highs returning back close to\nnormal by Monday.\n\nTuesday through the middle of next week forecast confidence\ndecreases as model spread increases considerably. Most notably\nthere is a lot of model disagreement on timing out when the\ncoastal trough will move inland, how quickly, and how deep the\ntrough will be. Typically models will trend toward the slower\nsolutions with these coastal lows. So, something moving through\nlate week is the current thinking. This next coastal trough will \nbring chances for rain, breezy winds, and cooler temperatures, but\nto what magnitude is still uncertain.",
